        {
          "content": "codex --model gpt-5.6-sol -c model_reasoning_effort=medium --sandbox workspace-write --ask-for-approval on-request --search exec --json",
          "id": "agent-turn-625d27fc-5272-49cb-ae58-8ed982a826f6-started",
          "kind": "event",
          "sequence": 2,
          "timestamp": "2026-08-04T17:40:00.563Z",
          "type": "started"
        },
        {
          "agent": "codex",
          "content": "I’ll review the feature document against the actual frontend/desktop architecture and trace every affected call path. I’ll report ambiguities, risks, and any steps that need tightening; I won’t modify files.\n\nThe document’s intent is coherent, but it deliberately omits the implementation sequence. I’m now checking the key concurrency boundary: conversation writes happen in the Electron activity writer, while popup visibility and acknowledgement indicators live in the React app. That boundary determines whether “later transcript persistence cannot overwrite a newer view state” is actually achievable.\n\nA key implementation constraint is now confirmed: simply adding `viewed` to the frontend conversation object is unsafe. The desktop writer currently replaces the entire stored conversation at both checkpoints and terminal completion. The writer must merge the currently persisted `viewed` value into every replacement inside the same per-file queue; otherwise an older run snapshot can revert a newer acknowledgement.\n\nThe feature is technically feasible, but I would not call the document fully implementation-ready yet. The main architecture is sound; five behavioral details should be clarified before coding because they materially affect the implementation.\n\nI reviewed the current worktree, including the uncommitted F_111 acknowledgement changes. I made no changes.\n\n## Material ambiguities\n\n1. Exact “user-relevant” statuses\n\nThe wording says “such as `waitingForInput`, `completed`, or `failed`,” which leaves the set open-ended.\n\nThe code also has `cancelled` and `okButNotAfter`, although conversations themselves do not support `okButNotAfter`.\n\nRecommended rule:\n\n- Mark unseen only on transitions to `waitingForInput`, `completed`, or `failed`.\n- Do not mark `cancelled` unseen.\n- Ignore action statuses that have no conversation.\n\nThis should be stated as an exact set rather than examples.\n\n2. A visible card chat exists outside `CardActionPopupService`\n\nThe list editor opens a card/file `ActionPopup` directly in [list_editor_toolbar_controls.tsx](C:/Users/janbo/Documents/dev/md2/app/src/components/text_view/list_editor_toolbar_controls.tsx), without registering it in `CardActionPopupService`.\n\nUnder F_140’s strict definition, that chat can be visibly rendered on screen but can never count as visible, because it has no service entry or stack position.\n\nOne of these rules is needed:\n\n- Only card-view popups managed by `CardActionPopupService` acknowledge conversations; the list-editor popup intentionally does not.\n- Or migrate the list-editor card popup into the service.\n- Or broaden visibility to include non-service popups.\n\nThe first matches the document literally but produces surprising UX.\n\n3. Waiting-for-approval can occur before a conversation exists on disk\n\nQuestion and ordinary waiting transitions persist a checkpoint before publishing the waiting event. Approval waiting does not: [agent_runner_service.js](C:/Users/janbo/Documents/dev/md2/desktop/src/actions/agent/agent_runner_service.js:948) publishes the state without first calling the checkpoint writer.\n\nTherefore:\n\n1. The frontend receives `waitingForInput`.\n2. It tries to persist `viewed: false`.\n3. The activity file may not contain that new conversation yet.\n4. The view update cannot find anything to modify.\n\nRecommended solution: persist a conversation checkpoint before publishing an approval waiting event, consistent with question handling. Otherwise the acknowledgement backend needs a considerably more complicated pending-update mechanism.\n\n4. Whether view-state writes should create Git commits\n\nThe serialized activity writer has two paths:\n\n- `updateActivity`: writes the file without committing.\n- `updateAndCommitActivity`: writes and commits.\n\n“Travel with its conversation log” suggests the value eventually needs to be committed and pushed, but the document only says to use the existing serialized writer.\n\nRecommended rule:\n\n- Acknowledgement changes use `updateActivity`, with no dedicated Git commit.\n- A later normal activity commit includes the view change.\n- If immediate cross-machine synchronization is required, the document must explicitly require a commit/push policy.\n\nCreating a Git commit for every popup visibility change would be noisy and should not be inferred.\n\n5. Multiple unseen conversations for one action\n\nThe new model stores view state per conversation. That differs from the existing timestamp checkpoint, where acknowledging the newest result implicitly acknowledges every older result.\n\nRecommended per-conversation behavior:\n\n- The selector opens the newest unseen conversation.\n- Viewing it marks only that conversation viewed.\n- If older unseen conversations remain, the action LED remains.\n- Opening the next unseen conversation eventually clears it.\n\nIf viewing the newest conversation should also mark older conversations viewed, that needs to be stated explicitly because it conflicts with the per-conversation model.\n\n## Implementation walkthrough\n\n### 1. Extend the canonical conversation format\n\nUpdate the shared parser in [agent_conversations.mjs](C:/Users/janbo/Documents/dev/md2/shared/agent_conversations.mjs):\n\n- Missing `viewed` becomes `true`.\n- A present value must be boolean; malformed values should fail rather than be coerced.\n- Return `viewed` on every parsed conversation.\n\nAdd `viewed: boolean` to `AgentConversation` in [data_types.ts](C:/Users/janbo/Documents/dev/md2/app/src/data/data_types.ts:299).\n\nBecause card activity parsing delegates to the shared conversation parser at [card_activity.mjs](C:/Users/janbo/Documents/dev/md2/shared/card_activity.mjs:127), this automatically supplies the compatibility behavior for old activity files.\n\nThe document says “card activity JSON,” but the parser is shared with project conversations. The clean implementation would normalize `viewed` on both kinds while only applying acknowledgement behavior to card conversations.\n\n### 2. Initialize new conversations as viewed\n\nAdd `viewed: true` when a new conversation is constructed in [agent_conversation.js](C:/Users/janbo/Documents/dev/md2/desktop/src/actions/agent/agent_conversation.js:53).\n\nWhen continuing an existing conversation:\n\n- Preserve its current `viewed`.\n- Starting another turn should not itself make it unseen.\n- A later relevant state transition decides whether it becomes unseen again.\n\nThis needs corresponding constructor/parser tests.\n\n### 3. Make replacement writes preserve current view state\n\nThe important concurrency fix belongs in [activity_files.js](C:/Users/janbo/Documents/dev/md2/desktop/src/actions/activity/activity_files.js:119).\n\n`upsertConversation` currently replaces the complete stored object with the run snapshot. It should instead:\n\n- Find the currently stored conversation.\n- For an existing conversation, copy the stored `viewed` onto the replacement.\n- For a new conversation, use the incoming `viewed`, which must be `true`.\n\nThis merge must occur inside `queueActivityUpdate`, not before entering the queue. Otherwise a stale checkpoint can still read an older value and overwrite a newer acknowledgement.\n\nThat protects both:\n\n- Transcript checkpoint writes.\n- Terminal conversation writes.\n\n### 4. Add one atomic view-state writer\n\nAdd a focused backend operation such as:\n\n```ts\nsetActivityConversationViewed(reference, viewed)\n```\n\nIt should:\n\n1. Parse the activity reference.\n2. Enter the existing per-file `activityWriteQueues` queue.\n3. Read and parse the latest file.\n4. Find the exact conversation.\n5. Change only its `viewed` property.\n6. Atomically write the activity file.\n7. Return the parsed updated conversation.\n\nIt should not accept project ID, project key, card path, or action ID. The activity reference already identifies the persisted conversation.\n\nThe bridge surface then needs matching additions in:\n\n- `ElectronActionBridge`\n- Electron preload’s action method allowlist\n- `local_bridge_dispatch`\n- `RemoteControlStorageService`\n- Their bridge/dispatch tests\n\nThis also keeps remote-control clients working.\n\n### 5. Replace the local-storage acknowledgement service\n\nThe current implementation in [agent_acknowledgement_service.ts](C:/Users/janbo/Documents/dev/md2/app/src/services/agents/agent_acknowledgement_service.ts) should be replaced rather than extended.\n\nRemove:\n\n- `md2.agentAcknowledgements`\n- Timestamp calculations and checkpoint comparisons\n- Project-key parameters\n- `renameCardPath`\n- The rename call from [data_service.ts](C:/Users/janbo/Documents/dev/md2/app/src/services/data/data_service.ts:342)\n\nThe replacement service should own transient acknowledgement overrides and scoped listeners.\n\nA suitable identity is:\n\n```text\ncardPath → actionId → conversationId → conversation/viewed state\n```\n\nAlthough notifications are card/action-scoped, conversation ID is still required internally because multiple unseen conversations may exist under one action.\n\nSuggested subscriptions:\n\n- `subscribeCard(cardPath, listener)` for the aggregate `CardRunButton`.\n- `subscribeAction(cardPath, actionId, listener)` for selector, picker, and chat leaves.\n\nA card/action update should notify:\n\n- The exact action subscribers.\n- The affected card’s aggregate subscribers.\n- Nothing else.\n\nIt must not call `refreshSnapshot`, `updateAgentConversation`, or dispatch a `ProjectCard` change.\n\n### 6. Observe relevant action state transitions\n\nThe service can subscribe to `actionRunRegistry.subscribeActiveRunEvents`.\n\nFor every event:\n\n1. Require a card context with `context.file`.\n2. Resolve the current conversation from the run store after the registry has applied the event.\n3. Ignore events without a conversation.\n4. Detect an actual transition into one of the configured relevant states.\n5. Determine whether that exact conversation chat is visible.\n6. Set the desired runtime state synchronously.\n7. Persist only if the desired value differs from the known value.\n\nTransition detection is important because repeated output/events carrying `waitingForInput` must not repeatedly write `viewed: false`.\n\nTerminal handling should use the `agentClosed` conversation because the desktop side has already finished its terminal persistence before publishing that event.\n\n### 7. Track popup visibility precisely\n\n`CardActionPopupService` already owns stack order in [card_action_popup_service.ts](C:/Users/janbo/Documents/dev/md2/app/src/services/actions/card_action_popup_service.ts:42). Its last entry is the topmost entry, and [activate](C:/Users/janbo/Documents/dev/md2/app/src/services/actions/card_action_popup_service.ts:88) moves an entry there.\n\nThe service needs a stable way to ask:\n\n```text\nIs popup entry X currently the topmost entry?\n```\n\nThe popup entry ID should be passed from `CardActionPopupHostEntry` down to the chat owner. The chat leaf already proves most of the visibility predicate:\n\n- It is mounted only while the popup is open.\n- Its `actionId` is the currently selected action.\n- It knows the conversation it is actually displaying.\n\nThe remaining check is whether its popup entry is topmost.\n\nThis is preferable to copying `stackPosition` into acknowledgement state, because positions change whenever a popup is activated or closed.\n\n### 8. Acknowledge when visibility changes\n\nThe chat owner should react when any of these changes:\n\n- Displayed conversation\n- Selected action\n- Popup opens\n- Popup is activated\n- Another popup opens above it\n- A higher popup closes\n\nIf the exact displayed conversation is unseen and its popup is now topmost, request `viewed: true`.\n\nImportant behavior:\n\n- Becoming covered does not make a conversation unseen.\n- Only a new relevant action-state transition makes it unseen.\n- A failed conversation load remains unseen because it was never displayed.\n- Selecting “no conversation” does not acknowledge anything.\n\nThe current unconditional completed-conversation effect in [action_conversation_chat.tsx](C:/Users/janbo/Documents/dev/md2/app/src/components/actions/action_conversation_chat.tsx:49) should be removed or reduced to presentation-only behavior. It does not know whether the popup is topmost.\n\n### 9. Derive indicators from persisted state plus runtime overrides\n\n`CardRunButton`, `ActionSelector`, and the conversation picker should use hooks subscribing directly to the new service.\n\nQueries should combine:\n\n- Conversations from the current card snapshot.\n- Live conversations retained by the acknowledgement service.\n- Runtime view overrides that may be newer than the snapshot.\n\nThis is necessary because the `ProjectCard` must deliberately remain stale after a view-only update.\n\nThe existing F_111 automatic-selection behavior in [action_conversation_store.ts](C:/Users/janbo/Documents/dev/md2/app/src/components/actions/action_conversation_store.ts:80) can remain, but it must select based on `conversation.viewed === false`, not timestamps.\n\n### 10. Handle write failures and races\n\nThe document does not specify failure behavior. I recommend:\n\n- Apply the desired state optimistically so indicators react immediately.\n- Serialize backend writes through the existing file queue.\n- Track the latest desired value per conversation.\n- On failure, revert only if no newer desired value has superseded the failed request.\n- Report the error through `dialogService`.\n- Leave/reinstate unseen state when marking viewed fails so it can be retried on the next visibility change.\n\nWithout the “latest desired value” check, a failed older `false` request could incorrectly roll back a later successful `true` request.\n\n### 11. Project switching\n\nRemoving project keys is safe only if runtime acknowledgement state is cleared on project/session replacement.\n\n`CardActionPopupService` already clears its entries when the project changes. The acknowledgement service should similarly clear its runtime map and listeners’ snapshots at that boundary, without incorporating the project identity into its keys or events.\n\nOtherwise identical card paths and action IDs can leak transient state across sequentially loaded projects.\n\n## Required tests\n\nAt minimum:\n\n- Parser defaults missing `viewed` to `true`.\n- Parser preserves explicit `true` and `false`.\n- Parser rejects a non-boolean `viewed`.\n- New conversations start viewed.\n- Continued conversations preserve view state.\n- Atomic backend updates change only the targeted conversation.\n- A checkpoint queued after an acknowledgement preserves the acknowledgement.\n- A terminal write queued after an acknowledgement preserves it.\n- Closed popup produces unseen state.\n- Lower popup produces unseen state.\n- Topmost matching popup remains viewed.\n- Topmost popup with another action selected produces unseen state.\n- Topmost popup displaying another conversation produces unseen state.\n- Activating the matching lower popup marks its conversation viewed.\n- Closing the covering popup marks the newly exposed conversation viewed.\n- Failed conversation load leaves it unseen.\n- Only the affected action and card aggregate listeners fire.\n- No `DataService` card-change or snapshot refresh occurs.\n- Project switching clears runtime overrides.\n- Multiple unseen conversations follow the chosen per-conversation rule.\n- Remote-control bridge forwards the new operation.\n- Approval-driven `waitingForInput` works when the conversation was previously absent from the activity file.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe architecture can support F_140 cleanly, especially because the activity writer is already serialized and popup stacking already has a single owner. Before implementation, I would clarify:\n\n1. The exact status set.\n2. Whether the list-editor popup counts as visible.\n3. Whether view writes create Git commits.\n4. How multiple unseen conversations behave.\n5. The expected rollback/retry behavior after persistence failure.\n\nThe approval checkpoint issue is not merely documentation ambiguity—it must be addressed in the implementation or `waitingForInput` acknowledgements will be unreliable.",
          "id": "agent-turn-625d27fc-5272-49cb-ae58-8ed982a826f6-assistant",
          "kind": "message",
          "role": "assistant",
          "sequence": 3,
          "timestamp": "2026-08-04T17:45:36.586Z"
        },
        {
          "content": "---\nauthor:\nid: F_140\ninternalId: 7b158d24-318d-4081-934b-b9255a0672dc\ntitle: persist conversation view state\nstatus: ready for implementation\nowner:\naffects:\nagents:\npolicy:\nafter: \n---\n\n## Problem\n\nConversation acknowledgements are stored in browser local storage as one timestamp per project and card. The state actually belongs to an individual card action conversation and should travel with its conversation log.\n\nUpdating this state must remain independent from card state updates so one acknowledgement does not re-render the entire card.\n\n## Requested changes\n\n- Store `viewed` on every conversation in the card activity JSON. Existing conversations without the field are read as `viewed: true`; new conversations also start viewed.\n- Set a conversation to not viewed when its action reaches a user-relevant state such as `waitingForInput`, `completed`, or `failed`, unless its chat is currently visible.\n- A chat is visible only when its popup is open, its action is selected, its conversation is displayed, and that popup has the highest `stackPosition` among the entries in `CardActionPopupService`.\n- Set an unseen conversation to viewed as soon as that chat becomes visible.\n- Keep acknowledgement updates as a separate card/action-scoped event. `CardRunButton` and popup leaves subscribe directly; do not refresh or republish the `ProjectCard`.\n- Scope runtime acknowledgement state by `cardPath` and `actionId`. Do not persist or pass a project key: only one project is loaded and events cannot cross projects.\n- Remove local-storage acknowledgements, timestamp checkpoints, and card-path rename migration.\n- Persist view changes through the existing serialized activity-file writer and preserve `viewed` when transcript checkpoints or terminal writes replace a conversation.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- View state is stored per conversation in its card activity JSON.\n- A result produced behind a closed, lower, or differently selected popup is marked unseen.\n- A result shown in the topmost matching popup remains viewed.\n- Bringing the matching popup to the front marks its displayed unseen conversation viewed.\n- Only acknowledgement subscribers for the affected card/action update; the card itself is not refreshed.\n- Existing conversation files load as viewed.\n- Later transcript persistence cannot overwrite a newer view state.\n\r\n---\ninternalId: 583d9c88-b71c-4560-92ad-3e23d33bf1d0\n---\n\n# Architectural decisions\n\nProject-wide decisions for MDÂ². The app is a Vite React web app in `app/`; `desktop/` is a separate Electron host that can load the website and expose local capabilities through preload bridges.\n\n## File naming\n\n| Decision | Rule | Rationale |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Code files use lowercase snake_case names. | All code files in `app/src/` and `desktop/` use lowercase words separated by underscores, for example `github_auth_service.ts`, `project_workspace.tsx`, and `local_git_service.js`. Test files keep the same base name with `.test` before the extension, for example `github_auth_service.test.ts`. Exported symbols keep normal TypeScript/JavaScript casing such as `GithubAuthService`, `ProjectWorkspace`, and `useGithubAuth`. | A single file naming convention avoids mixed PascalCase, camelCase, and hyphenated filenames, and keeps imports predictable across Windows and case-sensitive environments. |\n\n## Services and state\n\n| Decision | Rule | Rationale |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Global singleton services own domain state and logic. | Each domain is a class in `src/services/**`, instantiated once at module level and exported, for example `export const indexesService = new IndexesService()`. React components do not own domain state. | Keeps business logic out of components, testable without a DOM, and shared across windows/views without Redux, Zustand, or Context stores. |\n| Service location follows usage scope. | If a service is specific to one UI element and used only there, place it beside that component. Generic or shared services, such as logging, database access, config, storage, or cross-screen domain logic, belong in `src/services/`. | Keeps component-owned behavior local while preserving `src/services/` for reusable application services. |\n| Consumers use direct imports or the service injector. | Direct import when no import cycle exists; use `src/services/service_injector.js` when a cycle would occur. Every global singleton service registers itself in its constructor with `inj.register('myService', this)`. | The service graph is dense; the injector is a bare global registry that defers resolution to call time. |\n| Services have a two-phase lifecycle. | Constructors only register and set unloaded state such as `null`. Config-dependent or service-dependent work runs in `init()`, `start()`, app bootstrap (`App.jsx`), or a `loaded` lifecycle event. `configService.config` is private; callers use `get`, `set`, and `clear`. | Module evaluation order is not guaranteed, so `configService` is not usable at import time. Reading config in a constructor is a bug. |\n\n\n## Events and React integration\n\n| Decision | Rule | Rationale |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Services notify observers through their own `EventTarget`. | Services expose an `EventTarget` and dispatch named `CustomEvent` domain events such as `activeIndex`, `added`, `deleted`, or `changedBox`. Observers subscribe with `addEventListener` and unsubscribe with `removeEventListener` on the owning service. | Decouples producers from consumers without depending on Node `EventEmitter`, which is not available in the browser-first React app. |\n| Custom hooks bridge service events to functional components. | Hooks in `src/components/hooks/`, such as `useActiveIndex` and `useProjectConfig`, seed `useState` from a service property, subscribe in `useEffect`, unsubscribe in cleanup, and return the value. Prefer hook + service over prop chains. | Components stay declarative and avoid direct event plumbing. |\n| Components subscribe at the smallest rendering boundary. | Root and layout components compose UI regions and pass structural inputs. Components that render changing application data subscribe through hooks themselves. Do not hoist changing data into ancestors only to pass it downward. | Limits rerenders to affected UI, protects transient state such as open popups, and avoids broad prop chains. |\n| Components use the app theme service path. | `App` owns theme mode through `useThemeMode()`, builds the MUI theme with `createAppTheme(mode)`, and provides it through `ThemeProvider`. Components read theme values with MUI APIs such as `useTheme`, `useMediaQuery`, and `sx`; only controls that change the palette mode receive `mode` and `onToggleTheme`. | Theme state, persistence, and palette construction stay centralized while components remain presentation-focused. |\n\r\n---\ninternalId: f2ec9e28-8064-4707-bce9-b62f24d57524\n---\n\n# App Style Guide â€” Kanban / MDÂ² (React + MUI)\n\nHow to build **any new component** so it matches the app. This is written for **Material UI v5+** components.\n\n**The theme is the source of truth.** All color, radius, base typography, and spacing units live in the single `createTheme` (defined in `GUIDANCE.md` Â§1/Â§9). This guide does **not** define colors â€” it tells you **which theme value to use for which role**, and covers the things the theme can't encode: layout, spacing/margins, borders, elevation, and component conventions.\n\n**The one rule that matters:** never write a raw hex, px radius, or magic color into a component. Read it from the theme (`theme.palette.*`, `theme.shape`, `theme.typography`, `theme.spacing`). If a component has a hardcoded hex in its `sx`/styled, it's wrong â€” that's exactly where the look drifts and where dark mode breaks.\n\n---\n\n## 1. How to consume the theme\n\n- **Colors:** `sx={{ color: 'text.secondary', bgcolor: 'background.paper', borderColor: 'divider' }}` â€” MUI resolves dotted palette paths automatically in `sx`. In `styled`/JS use the callback form: `styled(Box)(({ theme }) => ({ color: theme.palette.text.secondary }))`.\n- **Spacing:** use the 8px unit via the spacing scale â€” `sx={{ p: 2, gap: 1, mt: 1.5 }}` (`2` = 16px, `1` = 8px). Don't write `padding: '16px'`.\n- **Radius:** `borderRadius: 1` (= `theme.shape.borderRadius` = 8) and multiples; for the fixed non-8 radii see Â§4.\n- **Typography:** prefer `Typography variant=...` and `theme.typography.*` over ad-hoc `fontSize`.\n- **Custom tokens:** roles the default MUI palette doesn't cover (column/track surfaces, the strong/hover border steps, `text-3/4`, section-header color, `primary-bg` tint, status colors) must exist in the theme under a namespace (e.g. `theme.palette.custom.*`) with light + dark values. Consume them the same way â€” `bgcolor: 'custom.column'`. **Never inline their hex.** If a needed token doesn't exist yet, add it to the theme, don't hardcode it in the component.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Role â†’ theme slot\n\nPick the slot by the element's **role**, not by how it looks in one mode:\n\n| Role | Theme slot |\n|---|---|\n| App background; tinted footer/action bars | `background.default` |\n| Column & sidebar surface | `custom.column` |\n| Segmented-control / toolbar track, hover fill | `custom.track` |\n| Card, dialog, popup, input surface | `background.paper` |\n| Hairlines, resting borders | `divider` |\n| Input borders, vertical separators | `custom.borderStrong` |\n| Border on hover | `custom.borderHover` |\n| Titles, values, headings | `text.primary` |\n| Body copy, field labels | `text.secondary` |\n| Meta, captions, resting icons | `custom.text3` |\n| Muted / placeholder / disabled | `custom.text4` |\n| Uppercase section/column headers | `custom.colHead` |\n| The one CTA, selection, focus, links, icon-hover | `primary.main` |\n| Contained-button hover | `primary.dark` |\n| Selected/tinted bg, focus ring, ID-chip bg | `custom.primaryBg` |\n| Text/icon on a primary fill | `primary.contrastText` |\n| Active / saved / online dot | `success.main` |\n| Destructive action | `error.main` |\n| Status swatch dots & ID-chip tints (decorative only) | `custom.status.*` |\n\nRules: **one** `primary` per surface (the CTA/selection accent) â€” everything else is `text-*`/`divider`. Status colors are **decoration only** (dots, chip tints), never interactive color. Dark-mode brightness/tinting is handled inside the theme's two palettes, so a correctly-sloted component needs zero per-mode code.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Layout\n\n- **Full-height app:** column flex â€” menu row / ribbon / content (`flex: 1; minHeight: 0`) / status bar. Fixed bands `flexShrink: 0`.\n- **Board:** horizontal-scroll flex row, `gap: 2` (16px), page padding `p: 2.5` (20px); columns `width: 296, flexShrink: 0`, internal column flex `gap: 1`.\n- **List view:** two-pane flex row filling the space between ribbon and status bar â€” sidebar `width: 280, flexShrink: 0`, editor pane `flex: 1, minWidth: 0` (the `minWidth: 0` is required so long content ellipsizes instead of blowing out the row).\n- **Panels (sidebar, editor card, dialogs):** column flex with stacked bands (header / [toolbar] / body / footer). The **body scrolls** (`overflow-y: auto; minHeight: 0`); header and footer are `flexShrink: 0` and never scroll away.\n- **Spacer pattern:** push trailing items with `<Box sx={{ flex: 1 }} />` rather than `margin-left: auto` sprinkled around, so rows stay predictable.\n- Use flex `gap` for spacing between siblings â€” don't hand-space with per-child margins.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Spacing, margin & size conventions\n\n- **4/8px grid, via `theme.spacing`.** page padding `2.5`; between columns `2`; inside columns / between related controls `1`; dialog body gap `2â€“2.25`; field labelâ†’control gap `~0.9` (7px).\n- **Margins:** prefer padding on containers + flex `gap` over element margins. Where a floating panel needs breathing room (e.g. editor card, toolbar inside a panel), use container padding (`p: 2 / 2.5`), not margins on the children.\n- **Fixed heights (px):** menu row 44, ribbon 52, status bar 32, section header 40, tab row 40, standard button / control 34, dense icon button 26â€“28, input 42.\n- **Fixed widths (px):** column 296, sidebar 280, search 260, standard dialog 480, wide/editor dialog 760.\n- **Radius family (px):** buttons/icon-buttons 8 (small 6â€“7), inputs 9, cards 10, columns/panels 12, dialogs/popups 14, chips/pills 99, color swatches 3. `borderRadius: 1` covers the 8 case; use explicit `'9px'` etc. for the others.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Borders & elevation\n\n- **Rest = border, not shadow.** Resting surfaces: `border: '1px solid'`, `borderColor: 'divider'`. Inputs use `custom.borderStrong`. **Shadow only when floating or lifted** (hover, drag, dialogs/popovers).\n- **Separation between bands:** a single 1px `divider` hairline. Don't stack a heavy rule *and* an outline â€” one line, let background contrast do the rest. Vertical separators inside toolbars: a 20px-tall `custom.borderStrong` rule.\n- **Elevation ladder (only where floating):** card hover `0 4px 12px rgba(16,24,40,0.12)`; dragging MUI `elevation 8` + `rotate(2deg)`; contained button `0 1px 2px rgba(16,24,40,0.15)`; dialog/popup `0 24px 60px rgba(16,24,40,0.28)`; scrim `rgba(16,24,40,0.45)` + `backdropFilter: blur(2px)`.\n- Set `MuiPaper.elevation: 0` and `MuiButton.disableElevation` globally in the theme; add the shadows above explicitly where the ladder calls for it.\n- **On dark backgrounds shadows read as nothing** â€” the theme already handles this by making hover raise `borderColor` (`custom.borderHover`) instead of relying on shadow.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Typography usage\n\nDrive everything from theme variants / `theme.typography`; avoid ad-hoc `fontSize`.\n\n- Titles/values/headings â†’ `text.primary`; body & labels â†’ `text.secondary`; meta â†’ `custom.text3`.\n- **Field labels:** a plain `Typography` (12px/600, `text.secondary`) placed **above** the control â€” **never** MUI floating/outlined `TextField` labels (they clash with the app's flat-label style).\n- **Section/column headers:** `Typography variant=\"overline\"` (uppercase, `letterSpacing ~0.7px`, `fontWeight 700`, `custom.colHead`).\n- **ID chips only** use monospace; everything else is the theme's default family.\n- `button.textTransform: 'none'` and `button.fontWeight: 600` are set in the theme â€” don't re-uppercase buttons.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Interaction states\n\nConsistent across all components; all colors from the theme:\n\n- **Ghost icon button** (`IconButton`): transparent â†’ hover `bgcolor: track` (or `background.paper` on a colored panel) + `color: primary.main`. Override MUI's default circular hover tint via `sx`.\n- **Outlined button:** `divider` border, `text.secondary` â†’ hover `borderColor/color: primary.main`. Destructive: hover `borderColor: error.main` + `bgcolor: custom.dangerBg`.\n- **Contained (primary) CTA:** `primary.main` + small shadow â†’ hover `primary.dark`.\n- **Input/field focus:** `borderColor: primary.main` + `boxShadow: 0 0 0 3px` in `custom.primaryBg` (the soft halo). Unfocused hover: `borderColor: custom.borderHover`.\n- **List/tree row:** hover `bgcolor: track`; selected `bgcolor: custom.primaryBg` + title `text.primary`.\n- **Row-level actions** (delete, â‹®): hidden at rest, shown on `:hover`/`:focus-within` via **opacity** (keep in DOM so width doesn't jump).\n- **Active tab/menu item:** 2px `primary.main` bottom border + `primary.main` text.\n- Every icon-only control gets a `Tooltip` (theme sets `arrow: true`) **and** an `aria-label`.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Component conventions (MUI primitives)\n\n- **Buttons:** `Button variant=\"contained\"` = the single CTA per surface; `variant=\"outlined\"` = secondary; `variant=\"text\"` = tertiary. Icon-only â†’ `IconButton` in a `Tooltip`. Don't use `Link` or text `Button` where an action button belongs (that reproduces the old text-link look).\n- **Icons:** `@mui/icons-material` **Outlined** variants; `fontSize: 18` in 34px buttons, `16` in dense, `13â€“15` inline. Don't hand-draw SVG.\n- **ID chip:** monospace, accent text on `custom.primaryBg`/status tint, `radius 5, flexShrink: 0`.\n- **Count chip:** `background.paper` bg, 1px `divider`, radius 9â€“10, `custom.text3`.\n- **State dot:** 7â€“8px circle + `text-3` label; green (`success.main`) = active/saved/online.\n- **Search:** pill `TextField` (`borderRadius: 99`, `bgcolor: background.default`), search start-adornment, `âŒ˜K` keycap end-adornment.\n- **Select:** `size=\"small\"`, optional dot+name via `renderValue`; 42px / radius-9 frame. When a select has a tooltip, keep the tooltip available while the select is closed and explicitly hide it for the entire time the select menu is open so it cannot cover the first option.\n- **Segmented control** (view switch, formatting toolbar): `ToggleButtonGroup` on a `custom.track` background; selected segment `background.paper` + `0 1px 2px` shadow + `primary` text.\n- **Dialog / popup:** `Dialog`/`Popover` with `PaperProps` â†’ `background.paper`, 1px `divider`, radius 14, big shadow; scrim via `slotProps.backdrop` (blur + tint). Bands separated by hairlines; footer on a `background.default` bar with top hairline; actions right-aligned (outlined Cancel + contained CTA). `autoFocus` first field; submit on `Enter` (`âŒ˜/Ctrl+Enter` in a textarea); close on `Esc`/backdrop.\n- **Empty state:** dashed target â€” `1.5px dashed custom.borderStrong`, radius 10, centered icon + `custom.text4` label. No \"click to add\" placeholder blocks.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Definition of done for a new component\n\n1. Zero hardcoded hex / raw px colors â€” every value comes from `theme.palette` / `theme.shape` / `theme.spacing` / `theme.typography`.\n2. Correct roleâ†’slot mapping (Â§2); at most one `primary` CTA on the surface.\n3. Borders at rest, shadow only when floating (Â§5); bands split by a single hairline.\n4. Hover **and** focus states on every interactive element (Â§7); icon buttons have tooltip + `aria-label`.\n5. Body scrolls, header/footer pinned; `minWidth: 0` on flex children that hold long text.\n6. Toggle to dark mode and confirm nothing breaks â€” if it does, you hardcoded a color somewhere (Â§1).\n\nComponent-specific detail lives in `GUIDANCE.md` (theme definition + board/list/dialog), `CARD_POPUP_GUIDE.md`, `RUN_DIALOG_GUIDE.md`, and `SIDEBAR_ACTIONS_GUIDE.md`.\n\r\n",
