can.resolve(records);\\\\\\\\n    166|         await concurrentRefresh;\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯[3/5]⎯\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n FAIL  src/git/worktree_service.test.mjs > WorktreeService lifecycle > reuses an active refresh so timer and callers cannot overlap scans\\\\\\\\nError: EBUSY: resource busy or locked, rmdir 'C:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Users\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\janbo\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\AppData\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Local\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\Temp\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\md2-worktrees-I5gzyY\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\primary'\\\\\\\\n⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯[4/5]⎯\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n FAIL  src/project/project_files.test.mjs > project-files > creates nested project and active folders with the project template\\\\\\\\nError: Test timed out in 5000ms.\\\\\\\\nIf this is a long-running test, pass a timeout value as the last argument or configure it globally with \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"testTimeout\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".\\\\\\\\n ❯ src/project/project_files.test.mjs:26:7\\\\\\\\n     24|\\\\\\\\n     25| describe('project-files', () => {\\\\\\\\n     26|     it('creates nested project and active folders with the project tem…\\\\\\\\n       |       ^\\\\\\\\n     27|         const rootPath = await mkdtemp(join(tmpdir(), 'md2-project-cre…\\\\\\\\n     28|\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯[5/5]⎯\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\n.\\\\\\\\design\\\\\\\\activity\\\\\\\\project.json:380:          \\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Your task is to prepare this project for development.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nInstructions:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. Start by locating and reading the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\`README.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\` file in the current directory.\\\\\\\\n2. Follow the setup instructions in that README exactly.\\\\\\\\n3. If the main README refers to additional \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\`README.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\` files in subdirectories, or if subdirectories contain setup instructions that are required for development, read those as well.\\\\\\\\n4. Perform all necessary project setup steps, including (where applicable):\\\\\\\\n   1. Installing dependencies.\\\\\\\\n   2. Installing toolchains or SDKs.\\\\\\\\n   3. Restoring packages.\\\\\\\\n   4. Creating generated files.\\\\\\\\n   5. Running bootstrap or setup scripts.\\\\\\\\n   6. Initializing git submodules.\\\\\\\\n   7. Installing workspace/package manager dependencies.\\\\\\\\n   8. Building any required generated artifacts.\\\\\\\\n5. Do not start long-running processes such as development servers or watch tasks unless the README explicitly requires them as part of setup.\\\\\\\\n6. If any step requires user input, elevated privileges, secrets, credentials, or external configuration, stop and explain exactly what is needed before proceeding.\\\\\\\\n7. If multiple package managers or setup methods are available, use the one recommended by the project's documentation.\\\\\\\\n8. If the documentation contains conflicting instructions, prefer the instructions in the top-level \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\`README.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\` and explain any conflict.\\\\\\\\n9. When setup is complete, provide a concise summary including:\\\\\\\\n   1. The steps that were performed.\\\\\\\\n   2. Any issues or warnings encountered.\\\\\\\\n   3. Any manual steps the developer still needs to complete.\\\\\\\\n   4. Whether the project appears ready for development.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDo not make assumptions beyond what is documented. Base your actions on the project's documentation whenever possible.\\\\\\\",\\\\n.\\\\\\\\design\\\\\\\\activity\\\\\\\\project.json:387:          \\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"I’ll inspect the repository’s setup instructions, package manifests, and current dependency state, then perform only the setup steps the project explicitly defines and verify the development checks that are available.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe repository defines three dependency sets: root tooling plus `app` and `desktop`. The documented `install:all` installs only the two subprojects, so I’m also checking the root tooling explicitly; telemetry `.env` files are optional and I will not create empty local copies.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nNode.js 22.16.0 is installed, but VS Code’s auto-attach debugger is injected into terminal Node processes and caused the first npm probe to stall. I’ll clear that injection only for setup commands, then install the locked dependencies in each project.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAll three dependency sets installed successfully. I’m now validating the prepared workspace with each subproject’s required lint and test commands; dependency deprecation notices came only from transitive Electron-side packages.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nLint and test validation is still running across both subprojects. No failures have been returned yet; the app test suite is the longer-running part.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe combined validation wrapper hit its timeout while the app’s Vitest process was still running, so it did not preserve the completed check outputs. I’m rerunning the checks independently with an appropriate test timeout so the final result is unambiguous.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nBoth linters pass cleanly. The desktop test runner is active but has not emitted per-file progress; I’m allowing the project’s full suite time to complete rather than treating the earlier wrapper timeout as a test failure.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe app suite passes all 1,223 tests. Desktop completed 447/451, with four failures concentrated in Git/filesystem tests: three hit the fixed 5-second timeout and one timing-sensitive worktree assertion then left a locked temp directory. I’m rerunning only those files with one worker to distinguish environmental contention from a real setup problem.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe isolated desktop rerun is still executing, which confirms this machine’s Git-backed tests are unusually slow relative to their 5-second per-test limits. I’m waiting for the final pass/fail output before deciding whether any project change is warranted.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe four previously failing desktop files pass when serialized. I’m now running the entire desktop suite with the same one-worker setting so the final development baseline reflects a complete passing suite, not just targeted reruns.\\\\\\\",\\\\n.\\\\\\\\design\\\\\\\\activity\\\\\\\\project.json:414:        \\\\\\\"output\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"I’ll inspect the repository’s setup instructions, package manifests, and current dependency state, then perform only the setup steps the project explicitly defines and verify the development checks that are available.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe repository defines three dependency sets: root tooling plus `app` and `desktop`. The documented `install:all` installs only the two subprojects, so I’m also checking the root tooling explicitly; telemetry `.env` files are optional and I will not create empty local copies.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nNode.js 22.16.0 is installed, but VS Code’s auto-attach debugger is injected into terminal Node processes and caused the first npm probe to stall. I’ll clear that injection only for setup commands, then install the locked dependencies in each project.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAll three dependency sets installed successfully. I’m now validating the prepared workspace with each subproject’s required lint and test commands; dependency deprecation notices came only from transitive Electron-side packages.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nLint and test validation is still running across both subprojects. No failures have been returned yet; the app test suite is the longer-running part.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe combined validation wrapper hit its timeout while the app’s Vitest process was still running, so it did not preserve the completed check outputs. I’m rerunning the checks independently with an appropriate test timeout so the final result is unambiguous.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nBoth linters pass cleanly. The desktop test runner is active but has not emitted per-file progress; I’m allowing the project’s full suite time to complete rather than treating the earlier wrapper timeout as a test failure.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe app suite passes all 1,223 tests. Desktop completed 447/451, with four failures concentrated in Git/filesystem tests: three hit the fixed 5-second timeout and one timing-sensitive worktree assertion then left a locked temp directory. I’m rerunning only those files with one worker to distinguish environmental contention from a real setup problem.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe isolated desktop rerun is still executing, which confirms this machine’s Git-backed tests are unusually slow relative to their 5-second per-test limits. I’m waiting for the final pass/fail output before deciding whether any project change is warranted.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe four previously failing desktop files pass when serialized. I’m now running the entire desktop suite with the same one-worker setting so the final development baseline reflects a complete passing suite, not just targeted reruns.2026-07-25T08:51:56.973877Z ERROR codex_models_manager::cache: failed to load models cache: missing field `supports_reasoning_summaries` at line 86 column 5\\\\\\\\n2026-07-25T08:52:40.596560Z ERROR codex_core::tools::router: error=Exit code: 124\\\\\\\\nWall time: 10.9 seconds\\\\\\\\nOutput:\\\\\\\\ncommand timed out after 10911 milliseconds\\\\\\\\nv22.16.0\\\\\\\\r\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2026-07-25T08:53:14.029034Z ERROR codex_core::tools::router: error=Exit code: 124\\\\\\\\nWall time: 1.6 seconds\\\\\\\\nOutput:\\\\\\\\ncommand timed out after 1567 milliseconds\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2026-07-25T08:54:48.320519Z ERROR codex_models_manager::manager: failed to renew cache TTL: missing field `supports_reasoning_summaries` at line 86 column 5\\\\\\\\n2026-07-25T08:56:59.605808Z ERROR codex_core::tools::router: error=Exit code: 124\\\\\\\\nWall time: 84 seconds\\\\\\\\nOutput:\\\\\\\\ncommand timed out after 83982 milliseconds\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n> app@0.0.0 test\\\\\\\\n> vitest run\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n RUN  v4.1.9 C:/Users/janbo/Documents/dev/md2_workers/dev1/app\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2026-07-25T08:57:48.485186Z ERROR codex_mode